Validation:
- `npm run typecheck` passes.
- `npm run check:schematic` completes with no `<SchematicPlacementIssues>`.
- `npm run build` succeeds in schematic-only mode. It still reports one connector-accessibility warning for the generic USB-C schematic connector, but the build exits successfully and writes the schematic output.

Design notes:
- Used built-in tscircuit primitives only; no tscircuit registry components.
- Modeled USB-C PD sink around `STUSB4500QTR`.
- Modeled LM3409 LED driver with power path, PFET switch, sense resistor, inductor, Schottky diode, LED ring connector, analog dimming filter, high-frequency PWM/EN input, I2C/debug header, and NTC input.
- Consulted official datasheets: ST STUSB4500 `https://www.st.com/resource/en/datasheet/stusb4500.pdf`, TI LM3409 `https://www.ti.com/lit/gpn/LM3409-Q1`.

**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**
- Confusing/surprising: `standard="usb_c"` matched the syntax primer but produced build-time “no ports” errors in this installed CLI version, so I replaced it with an explicit generic connector for schematic reliability.
- Biggest time sink: schematic placement cleanup. Some checker-suggested `schWidth` values fixed one warning while creating label collisions, and one combination stalled the checker.
- Helpful docs/tooling changes: clearer supported footprint strings, a machine-readable “apply placement fixes” command, and docs for when `connections` warnings are expected with `routingDisabled`.
- Specific friction points: `supplierPartNumbers` shape expects arrays, passive `schDisplayValue` is not accepted, `+`/`-` in pin labels failed validation, and the parts engine auto-selected JLC matches even after explicit supplier bindings were removed until build was run with `--disable-parts-engine`.
